          <caption>
            <p>Thoracic Level Kyphosis in Adolescent&#x000a0;With TB Spondylitis. Image of a 14-year-old boy presenting with severe thoracic kyphosis following tuberculous spondylitis (A). Sagittal MRI of the same patient demonstrating destruction of the T7&#x02013;T8 vertebrae with gibbus formation and compression of the intervertebral discs and bilateral intervertebral foramina at T6&#x02013;T7 and T7&#x02013;T8 (B). Juslin NP, Setiabudi D, Nataprawira H, Ramdan A. Clinical presentation of pediatric tuberculous spondylitis in high TB burden setting before and during the COVID-19 pandemic. <italic toggle="yes">J Infect in Dev Ctries.</italic> 2025;19(2):221-226. doi: 10.3855/jidc.20075.</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="A-Clinical-photograph-of-a-14-year-old-boy-presenting-with-severe-thoracic-kyphosis" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-27579.s18">
        <fig id="article-27579.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>CT and MRI Scans of TB Spondylitis Sagittal CT images indicate bony destruction in the C5, C6, T3, T10, T12, and L1 vertebrae (A&#x02013;C). Preoperative sagittal MRI shows high signal intensity in multiple vertebrae and a fusiform paraspinal abscess anterior to T1&#x02013;T4 (D, E). Axial MRI at the T4 level shows a paravertebral abscess (white arrow) [F]. Axial CT and MRI scans at the L4/5 intervertebral level indicate a massive psoas abscess (white arrow) [G, H]. Gao S, Hu Y, Sheng W. Multifocal noncontiguous spinal tuberculosis. <italic toggle="yes">Am J Trop Med Hyg.</italic> 2023;28;109(5):983-984. doi: 10.4269/ajtmh.23-0060.</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Screenshot__2025-12-03__at__1.11.26xPM" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
